r over four. I think that part's correct. I don't think you got the top correct though.\nTurn 68. TUTOR: So, you did the the first part correct, right? So you know that four times one is four. What's three times three?\nTurn 69. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 27 seconds]\nTurn 69. TUTOR: What you think?\nTurn 70. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 18 seconds]\nTurn 70. TUTOR: You think it's D?\nTurn 71. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds]\nTurn 71. TUTOR: Should I put D?\nTurn 72. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 73. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 74. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - clicks 'D' and 'Check'] Tutor selects option D and clicks the \"Check\" button. A message appears: \"That's incorrect. Did you multiply both the top and bottom of the fraction? Remember, only multiply the top part by the number.\"\nTurn 74. TUTOR: Let's try again.\nTurn 75. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - writing] Tutor erases the previous writing and writes \"3/1\" on the whiteboard.\nWrites \"3/1 x 3/4 =\" on the whiteboard.\nTurn 75. TUTOR: Let's do it this way, right? So let's say three times three over one times four.\nTurn 76. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 28 seconds]\nTurn 76. TUTOR: You think it's one?\nTurn 77. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds] Circles the fraction 3/4 in the problem on the screen.\nTurn 77. TUTOR: Did you read their note? They said, did you multiply both the top and bottom of the fraction? Remember, only multiply the top part by the number.\nTurn 78. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - drawing] Student circles the answer choice B, which is 9/4.\nTurn 78. STUDENT: Yeah.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nFor this next reply, decide privately which of the following teaching policies fits the current learner evidence, then apply it. Use the current mathematical task and recent student work in the context of the full conversation. Agreement with a tutor-supplied answer is not proof of understanding. An independently corrected earlier error does not by itself justify more help.\nSCAFFOLDING: Provide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nRIGOR: Push for appropriate rigor now. Build on what the learner has actually demonstrated, not an answer the tutor supplied. Invite the student to justify a step, connect representations, check a claim, generalize, or take the next step independently. Choose one opportunity that fits the current problem. Do not give the reasoning before asking for it, turn the challenge into a sequence of tiny leading hints, or introduce unrelated difficulty. Keep the mathematical work with the learner; rigor is not simply saying why after every answer.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
